AUDIO: 'Citadel of its time. Whenever an ape like you or me would buy a stock from a brokerage like E*TRADE, the brokerage didn\'t actually execute the trade themselves. Instead, they\'d often pass it off to Knight, who would fill the order and pocket the spread. At its core'

[01:26] [scene:visual]
VISUAL: The year "2012" displayed prominently.
AUDIO: 'was a system called SMARS, which was an order router that took large orders and chopped them up into smaller orders to fill them across the market at the best prices. It was fast, reliable, and more importantly, it printed money for over a'

[01:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'decade. Then, in the summer of 2012, the New York Stock Exchange decided it was sick of watching firms like Knight skim off all the retail orders before they ever reached the actual exchange. So, it created something called the retail'

